Match every nub in place, and publish the root volume on this thread

Run 461 opened the chain and also said what was left over.  It opened it
because the root nub was matched in place: the panel showed ArmDTPlatform
matched, the NioUfs personality attached, the driver's start entered and
the root path began.  What was left over is that the root nub was the
only service that was matched in place, so every other nub still took the
asynchronous path and queued its job for the configuration thread that
never runs.  The panel counted the rounds -- S #28, the last one B29
ArmDTIP -- and none of those nubs was matched.

So the fix from run 461 is widened.  Every nub is matched in place, on the
calling thread, which is what the synchronous branch of startMatching()
already does and what the code did before the asynchronous path existed.
The asynchronous path and the configuration thread are kept, because they
are the design; what changes is the choice between them.  A flag records
whether the configuration thread has ever been seen to run, the thread
sets it on its way in, and until it has, the match is done in place.  A
board where the thread works takes the asynchronous path again.

doServiceMatch() sets kIOServiceConfigRunning and clears
kIOServiceNeedConfigState, so the guard at the top of startMatching()
keeps a second round for the same nub from happening, and the matching
this brings in -- a driver that attaches, registers its own nubs and has
them matched -- is the chain the synchronous branch of upstream XNU runs
too.

The publication of the root volume has the same problem and the same
answer.  NioUfs publishes its windows from a thread it starts with
kernel_thread_start, and a thread that kernel_thread_start creates does
not run on this board: run 459 showed the configuration thread created
with a real handle ("CFG mk ok") and its body never entered ("CFG alive"
never appeared).  The publication is therefore done on the calling thread.
The retain before it and the release at the end of publishThread still
balance, because publishThread is entered the same way.

The prints around it say where the publication stands, because the driver
reports its failures with IOLog and IOLog does not reach the screen on
this board, while the driver's printf does.

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
index 8f58cf9..2e4583a 100644
--- a/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
@@ -97,6 +97,11 @@ static void nio_u_mark(const char *s);
 static void nio_u_seq(void);
 static unsigned nio_svc_seq;
 
+/* nio: whether the configuration thread has ever been seen to run.
+ * It is set by the thread itself on its way in, so until it has
+ * run, every nub is matched in place. */
+static volatile bool nio_config_thread_works = false;
+
 OSDefineMetaClassAndStructors(IOService, IORegistryEntry)
 
 OSDefineMetaClassAndStructors(_IOServiceNotifier, IONotifier)
@@ -1242,7 +1247,7 @@ IOService::startMatching( IOOptionBits options )
 			 * what the synchronous branch below does.  doServiceMatch()
 			 * sets kIOServiceConfigRunning, so the guard at the top of
 			 * this function keeps a second round from happening. */
-			if (getProvider() == NULL) {
+			if (!nio_config_thread_works) {
 				printf("\nnio: SM inline %s", getName() ? getName() : "?");
 				doServiceMatch( options );
 				printf("\nnio: SM inline done");
@@ -6138,6 +6143,7 @@ _IOConfigThread::main(void * arg, wait_result_t result)
 	bool            alive = true;
 	printf("\nnio: CFG alive");
 	nio_u_mark("\nALIVE");
+	nio_config_thread_works = true;
 	kern_return_t   kr;
 	thread_precedence_policy_data_t precedence = { -1 };
 
